# ============================================================
# langchain - manual API tests for LangChain concept demos
#
# Prerequisites:
#   1. pnpm dev:langchain   -> Express API on :3000
#   2. .env with OPENAI_API_KEY
#
# Each request maps 1:1 to a LangChain concept route. Run with
# "Send Request" (VS Code REST Client). /stream needs the
# streaming toggle (or -N via curl) to see live tokens.
# ============================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# 0. Index
# Verifies the server is up and lists every concept route.
# Expected 200: { "name": "langchain", "routes": [...] }
# ------------------------------------------------------------
GET http://localhost:3000/

###

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. /messages - message roles + hosted web search tool
# Concept: SystemMessage/HumanMessage are role-tagged, not raw
# strings. The model also gets OpenAI's hosted web_search tool,
# which runs server-side and returns results inline in content.
# Expected: { "roles": ["SystemMessage","HumanMessage"],
#             "response": "...", "toolCalls": [...],
#             "cacheRead": 0, "cacheCreation": 0 }
# ------------------------------------------------------------
POST http://localhost:3000/messages
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "message": "Vector databases are specialized database systems designed to store, index, and query high-dimensional vector embeddings. Unlike traditional relational databases that organize data into rows and columns with strict schemas, vector databases treat each record as a point in a high-dimensional space. This design makes them exceptionally well suited for similarity search, which is the computational foundation of modern retrieval-augmented generation systems. The core idea behind a vector database is straightforward: convert unstructured content such as documents, images, or audio into dense numerical vectors using an embedding model, then store those vectors alongside their original content. When a user submits a query, the same embedding model transforms the query into a vector, and the database finds the stored vectors that are geometrically closest to it. Closeness is typically measured using cosine similarity, Euclidean distance, or dot product, depending on the embedding model that was used during ingestion. Similarity search at scale is a hard problem. A naive approach that compares the query vector against every stored vector, known as a brute-force scan, works fine for small datasets but becomes prohibitively slow as the collection grows to millions or billions of vectors. To solve this, vector databases rely on approximate nearest neighbor algorithms, most notably Hierarchical Navigable Small World graphs and Inverted File indexes with Product Quantization. These techniques trade a small amount of recall accuracy for dramatic gains in search speed, often returning results in milliseconds even on very large collections. The indexing strategy matters a great deal in practice. HNSW builds a multi-layer graph where each layer contains a progressively sparser set of nodes, allowing searches to start at the top layer and descend toward the most relevant region of the space. IVF-PQ, by contrast, partitions the vector space into clusters and then compresses each vector into a compact code, which drastically reduces the memory footprint. Many production systems combine these approaches or layer additional filters on top, such as metadata filtering, hybrid search, and re-ranking pipelines. Hybrid search is an increasingly important feature. Pure vector search excels at semantic similarity but can miss exact keyword matches, while traditional full-text search such as BM25 excels at lexical matching but struggles with synonyms and paraphrases. Hybrid retrieval runs both searches in parallel and then fuses the results using algorithms like Reciprocal Rank Fusion, which combines the ranking positions from each method into a single unified score. This approach consistently improves retrieval quality across a wide range of benchmarks and is now a standard feature in many vector database products. Retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG, is the most prominent application of vector databases in the era of large language models. The pipeline works in stages. First, a corpus of documents is chunked into manageable pieces, embedded, and loaded into the vector store. At query time, the user's question is embedded and used to retrieve the most relevant chunks. Those chunks are then injected into a prompt alongside the original question, and a language model generates an answer grounded in the retrieved evidence. This grounding dramatically reduces hallucinations and allows the model to answer questions about private or recent information that was never part of its training data. Chunking strategy is one of the most impactful decisions in a RAG pipeline. Chunks that are too small lose surrounding context and produce fragmented answers, while chunks that are too large dilute the relevance signal and waste context window space. Common strategies include fixed-size chunking with overlap, recursive character splitting, and semantic chunking that respects natural boundaries such as paragraphs and sections. The optimal chunk size depends on the nature of the source documents and the capabilities of the embedding model, and it is almost always worth tuning empirically. Embedding models themselves have evolved rapidly. Early models produced fixed-length vectors with modest dimensionality, while modern models such as OpenAI's text-embedding-3-small produce 1536-dimensional vectors that capture surprisingly rich semantic structure. The choice of embedding model determines the dimensionality of the vectors, which in turn affects storage costs and search performance. Some databases support dimensionality reduction, quantization, and Matryoshka representation learning to compress embeddings without losing too much fidelity. Operational concerns round out the picture. Production vector databases must handle concurrent writes and reads, support incremental updates without full re-indexing, provide durable storage, and scale horizontally across multiple nodes. They also need to expose a clean query interface, ideally with support for filtering, pagination, and aggregation. Many teams choose a managed vector database service to avoid the operational burden of running a distributed index themselves, while others embed an open-source library directly into their application for simplicity. In summary, vector databases sit at the intersection of information retrieval, machine learning, and distributed systems. They turn the abstract idea of semantic similarity into a practical, queryable primitive that powers search, recommendation, anomaly detection, and generative AI applications. Understanding how they index, search, and scale is essential for anyone building modern AI systems, and the field continues to evolve rapidly as new algorithms and hardware capabilities emerge. One more consideration is the relationship between vector databases and caching. When a system repeatedly processes the same or similar prompts, the embedding and retrieval steps can become a bottleneck. Caching the embeddings of frequently used documents, or caching the final retrieved context for identical queries, can dramatically reduce latency and cost. This is especially relevant in RAG systems where the same corpus is queried many times, and where the cost of embedding large documents is non-trivial. A well-designed caching layer sits between the user and the retrieval pipeline, storing both intermediate representations and final answers, and it can be the difference between a system that feels instant and one that feels sluggish under load.",
  "search": false
}


